course: dictionary
lesson: 12608
title: Formal words for rude
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are more formal than others. Formal words are often used in letters, rules and speeches. Discourteous is a formal word that means rude. It means not polite, showing a lack of good manners or respect for other people.
explain: «Jack kept interrupting his teacher and never said sorry.» Interrupting someone who is speaking shows a lack of respect. The clue is that Jack did not wait his turn. Swap in rude and the sentence still makes sense, so discourteous means rude.
explain: Discourteous means the same as rude, but it sounds more careful and grown-up. A school rule might say, «Pushing past people in the line is discourteous.» That is a sensible way of saying you must not be rude in the queue.
fact: Discourteous means rude: not polite, or lacking good manners.
fact: Discourteous is a formal word, so you often find it in letters, rules and speeches.
fact: Interrupting, ignoring and pushing past people are all examples of discourteous behaviour.
fact: More discourteous is the comparison word: «Her reply was more discourteous than his.»
fact: The opposite of discourteous is courteous, which means polite and considerate.
exercise: «It was discourteous to push past the old lady in the queue.» What does discourteous mean? | *Rude and not polite | Very tired | Quick and clever | Kind and helpful
exercise: Which word means the same as discourteous? | *Rude | Happy | Polite | Ancient
exercise: «Ignoring a friend who is talking to you is discourteous.» Why is it discourteous? | Because you are hungry | Because you are very clever | Because you have a new coat | *Because it shows a lack of respect
test: Which sentence uses discourteous correctly? | *«Mia kept talking over the speaker, which was discourteous.» | «He walked to the park with his dog.» | «The cake was luminous and warm.» | The weather was discourteous today, with grey clouds and drizzle.
test: Which word is the opposite of discourteous? | Glum | *Courteous | Rude | Ancient
exam: Max writes in a letter, «Your behaviour at the party was discourteous.» What is Max saying? | *He thinks you were rude | He thinks you were famished | He thinks you were lost | He thinks you were very happy
